    "case_summary": "A 53-year-old Hispanic man with no prior comorbidities presented with a two-week history of recurrent cough, nonmassive hemoptysis, and central chest pain. One month earlier, he had mild COVID-19 not requiring hospitalization. He also reported fatigue, myalgias, and anorexia but denied fever, dyspnea, or gastrointestinal symptoms.\n\nOn examination, he was tachycardic but in no acute distress; the remainder of the exam was unremarkable. Initial laboratory studies showed leukocytosis (25.56×10^3/μL), blood urea nitrogen 107 mg/dL, creatinine 5.8 mg/dL (baseline unknown), mild transaminitis, hyponatremia, and hypoalbuminemia. Urinalysis and urine indices were ordered.\n\nA chest radiograph demonstrated bilateral scattered infiltrates with a denser consolidation in the right upper lobe. Noncontrast chest CT revealed multiple peripheral patchy consolidations in both lungs, some cavitating. These findings were interpreted as multifocal pneumonia.\n\nHe received intravenous azithromycin and ceftriaxone, intravenous bicarbonate, and emergent hemodialysis for acute kidney injury. Blood, sputum, and urine cultures were obtained, and PCR testing for SARS-CoV-2 was negative.\n\nInfectious workup results in the setting of acute kidney injury with pulmonary–renal disease are shown in Table 2.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. ANCA-associated vasculitis\nB. Community\nC. Pulmonary Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection\nD. Goodpasture’s disease (anti\nE. Invasive fungal disease\nF. ANCA\nG. None\nH. None\n",
